---
/**
 * Rewrite 2026-08 from owner-supplied project facts. Standard tier: the
 * previous page ran ~2,030 words and 14 media, and the >50% cut to ~850
 * words and 5 media is explicitly approved.
 *
 * Internal figures are published directionally (adoption, savings, per-visit
 * cost, store ratings) per the owner's publication rule; the Series B is the
 * one exact public figure and carries its source link.
 *
 * The 4 source videos are the "native clip" pattern — pre-existing .mp4 with
 * no .webm/poster sibling — so they pass `controls webm={false}
 * poster={false}` (see ContentMedia's doc comment).
 */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ContentBlock from '../../components/ContentBlock.astro';
import ContentMedia from '../../components/ContentMedia.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Impress App — remote aligner monitoring case study · Jury Alyavdin"
  description="The Impress app moved the fortnightly aligner check-up out of the clinic and onto the patient's phone — shipped in three months, with a clinician approving every step."
  path="/casestudy/impress-app.html"
  h1="Moving the fortnightly check-up from the clinic chair to a phone camera"
  avatarImage="/casestudy/impress-app/logo.png"
  orbitTop="Impress"
  orbitBottom="Orthodontic service"
  glow="#f8d8d8"
  heroImage="/casestudy/impress-app/hero.png"
  heroFit="contain"
  details={[
    { label: 'Platform', value: 'iOS, Android' },
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Design Lead, 3D modeler' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2021–2023' },
  ]}
>
  <ContentBlock title="Overview">
    <p>
      Impress runs its own chain of orthodontic clinics across Europe, straightening teeth with
      clear aligners instead of braces — more than 50,000 patients as of 2023. I was Design Lead
      on its patient app from 2021 to 2023, and the app did one main thing: it took the
      fortnightly check-up out of the clinic and put it on the patient's phone. The first version
      shipped in three months and is still live, largely as designed.
    </p>
    <p>
      I wrote the research script, designed the app, built the 3D models behind its instructional
      tutorials, and settled the clinical rules with the orthodontists who sign off on every case.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Problem">
    <p>
      Treatment runs in fortnightly steps — Impress said results could show in as little as six
      months — and each step needs a professional to confirm the teeth have actually moved before
      the patient switches to the next aligner. In practice that confirmation meant a clinic
      visit. Once acquisition and clinic overheads were counted, those appointments carried real
      cost for the company, and clinic capacity was being spent on visits that lasted minutes.
      Patients paid in time: one described it, roughly, as taking a day off work for five minutes
      in a chair.
    </p>
    <p>This is the appointment in question — a clinician, a scanner, and a chair, repeating every fortnight for the length of the treatment:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/patient.jpeg"
      alt="A gowned clinician using an intraoral scanner on a patient seated in a clinic chair"
      width={2000}
      height={1333}
    />
    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />
    <p>Consultation, plan, manufacturing and fitting happen once; the check-up in the middle repeats for the length of the treatment:</p>
    <a href="/casestudy/impress-app/journey01.png" aria-label="Open the full-size treatment lifecycle diagram">
      <ContentMedia
        src="/casestudy/impress-app/journey01.png"
        alt="Six-stage diagram: consultation, custom plan, manufacturing, onboarding, check-ups, duration"
        width={2600}
        height={1608}
      />
          <span class="media-note">Open full size</span>
    </a>
    <p>
      I wrote a structured interview script and the call centre ran it live with existing
      patients, on the phone, in Spanish. Five things came back consistently: the fortnightly
      check-ups disrupted people's schedules, waiting rooms ate time even with an appointment,
      patients wanted to watch their own progress, they were unsure when they were allowed to
      switch aligners, and booking was harder than it needed to be.
    </p>
    <p>Mapping the treatment stage by stage is what turned those complaints into a shape — pain at every step from the first visit to the final fitting, with the recurring clinic visit running through all of it:</p>
    <a href="/casestudy/impress-app/journey02.png" aria-label="Open the full-size journey map">
      <ContentMedia
        src="/casestudy/impress-app/journey02.png"
        alt="Journey map with patient quotes and pain points across every stage of treatment"
        width={2880}
        height={1188}
      />
          <span class="media-note">Open full size</span>
    </a>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="The photograph was the design problem, not the app">
    <p>
      The obvious move was to let patients photograph their own teeth and have a clinician review
      the images remotely. The orthodontists' objection was not about tooling. Their licence rests
      on what they can assess, and as one of them put it, roughly: I can't sign off on what I can't
      examine.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Rejected: keeping every check-up in the clinic.</strong> It was
      safe, and it was what the clinics already did — but it meant the service could only grow by
      adding chairs, and patients kept paying in half-days off work.
    </p>
    <p>
      That made the photograph the real problem. A clinically usable photo needs specific angles,
      a cheek retractor held in place, and enough light — taken by an untrained person, in a
      bathroom, on a phone. A picture that misses those is worthless to the clinician reviewing
      it, however good the software around it is. So the app's actual job was teaching capture.
    </p>
    <p>The capture flow guides each shot the clinician needs, then plays it back against the last one:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/sol04.png"
      alt="Retractor-guided photo capture screens beside a before-and-after comparison view"
      width={2320}
      height={1240}
    />
    <p>I modelled the kit in 3D so the tutorials could show each step from the angle a patient actually sees:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      controls
      webm={false}
      poster={false}
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/sol02"
      alt="Onboarding tutorial animating how to fit and change an aligner"
      width={1280}
      height={684}
    />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="A clinician approves every step">
    <p>
      With photos arriving reliably, a cheaper design came within reach: let the app judge the
      images and tell patients when to advance. It would have been faster for the patient and
      cheaper for the company, and it is what a pure software product would have done.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Rejected: letting the app advance patients on its own.</strong> A
      wrong call about tooth movement is a clinical harm, not a poor experience, and nobody on the
      clinical side would carry that.
    </p>
    <p>
      So every transition waits on a clinician. The patient uploads, the orthodontist reviews, and
      only then does the next aligner become available. Patient photographs are health data —
      special-category data under GDPR Article 9 — which set the terms for moving them from a phone
      to a clinician's screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other half of the loop was evidence. Aligners only work if they are worn around 22 hours
      a day, the standard clinical protocol, so the app logs wear time and reminds patients —
      giving the reviewing clinician something firmer than recollection to go on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two rounds of testing with existing patients shaped it, and the second is where the request
      for personal progress tracking came from. The daily log is what answered it — it turns "have
      you worn them?" into something a clinician can see:
    </p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      controls
      webm={false}
      poster={false}
      src="/casestudy/impress-app/sol03"
      alt="Daily wear-time log with a timer and reminders to keep aligners in"
      width={1280}
      height={684}
    />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Outcome">
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Shipped in three months, then every fortnight:</strong> a large share
      of patients were on it within months, and most were using it within six. Taking those
      appointments out of the clinics produced net savings in the low hundreds of thousands of
      euros a year once development costs were counted, and the app was well rated on both stores.
      In July 2022 Impress raised a{' '}
      <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2022/07/11/barcelonas-impress-heads-towards-a-125m-series-b-round-in-its-bid-to-digitize-orthodontics">$125 million Series B</a>{' '}
      — company context rather than the app's doing. Figures from Impress's internal reporting are
      given directionally here.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">What it cost:</strong> the patients who most needed a chair. The workflow was built around the common
      case, and treatments that genuinely needed hands-on assessment were served worse by a
      process designed for a photograph. Catching those cases early, instead of waiting for a
      clinician to spot them at review, is where I would start if I picked this up again.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>
</CaseStudyLayout>
